    "result_text": "Tests are green, and the customerCode is byte-for-byte identical (`NSW-16E6609E`), confirming the refactor preserved behaviour.\n\n## What I changed\n\nI agree with the finding \u2014 it's a genuine duplication of the exact \"single definition of X\" idiom this model layer is built on. Fixed it:\n\n- **`OwnerTelephones`** \u2014 added `toE164OrRaw(String)`, promoting the E.164-or-raw-fallback (empty when null) into the class that already owns the telephone-normalization fact.\n- **`OwnerIdentity.of()`** \u2014 now calls `OwnerTelephones.toE164OrRaw(...)` instead of its own inline copy.\n- **`AssignCustomerCode`** \u2014 now derives the hash's `normalizedTelephone` from the same `toE164OrRaw(...)`, and the hand-copied private `normalizedTelephone()` helper (with its \"mirroring OwnerIdentity\" caveat) is gone.\n\nNow the identityKey's telephone component and the customerCode hash input both derive \"normalized telephone\" from one definition, mirroring how `identityRegion` unifies the region/locality value. The self-contained `hash8` helper stays in `AssignCustomerCode` as the review noted it's appropriate there. Acceptance behaviour is unchanged (same customerCode output, `BUILD SUCCESS`, 1/1 test passing).",
